WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.820
- When the 21-year-old Prince

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.475
performed on "American Bandstand,"

00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:05.490
host Dick Clark told him that the song

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.670
he just played wasn't the kinda music

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.960
that comes from Minneapolis, Minnesota.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.630
- Yeah, I mean, this is
not the kind of music

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.680
that comes from Minneapolis, Minnesota.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.920
- I'm in Minneapolis, my hometown,

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.243
to meet with Jellybean Johnson,

00:00:17.243 --> 00:00:20.010
one of the pioneers of
the electro-funk style

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:21.690
known as the Minneapolis sound.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.570
- That's nasty, LA, that's nasty.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.420
- We're gonna prove Dick Clark wrong.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.200
I need to get some shiny
joints like the sequin diddies,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.687
'cause them is really hittin' right now.

00:00:30.687 --> 00:00:31.520
(laughing)

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:32.760
Okay, here we go.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.898
The Minneapolis sound is all about fusion,

00:00:34.898 --> 00:00:38.500
not only of genres, but of
black and white musical takes.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.310
The style changed the
course of pop in the '80s

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.972
with Prince and influenced
artists like Janet Jackson.

00:00:43.972 --> 00:00:46.639
(Nasty playing)

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.647
It can still be heard in pop music today.

00:00:50.647 --> 00:00:52.643
(Uptown Funk playing)

00:00:52.643 --> 00:00:55.190
♪ Doh, doh, doh, doh,
doh, doh, doh, doh, ha ♪

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:56.160
- I was blessed to work with

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.922
a Minneapolis music
legend, Jellybean Johnson,

00:00:58.922 --> 00:01:01.690
to create a Minneapolis
sound inspired track.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.490
- See, back in the day, I
would've played it like that.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:04.370
- So straight?
(drumming rhythmically)

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:05.370
- Uh-huh, I would play it like this.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.203
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:06.203 --> 00:01:08.280
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.680
- This man is the real deal.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.100
He was the drummer for the
Minneapolis band, "The Time."

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.060
He also worked with
legendary production team,

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.840
Jimmy Jam and Terry Lewis and
he even produced one of my

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.113
favorite Minnesota bands of
all time, "Mint Condition."

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:22.887
♪ Quit breaking my heart ♪

00:01:22.887 --> 00:01:25.940
But first, to understand what
the Minneapolis sound is,

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.360
we have to look at where it came from.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.120
Most people immediately think of Prince

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.090
when they think of the Minneapolis sound

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:32.975
and there's a good reason.

00:01:32.975 --> 00:01:37.017
His 1980 hit album, "Dirty
Mind," put the style on the map.

00:01:37.017 --> 00:01:38.545
♪ Uptown ♪

00:01:38.545 --> 00:01:40.500
♪ It's where I wanna be ♪

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.719
- He's the only cat I knew
that could take two strings

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:45.220
and it sound wide, you know?

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:46.670
It's be big as a house.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:47.503
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:47.503 --> 00:01:48.350
- You know, the rhythm
would be big as a house.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:49.919
Think about the rhythm
guitar in controversy.

00:01:49.919 --> 00:01:50.752
- [LA] Okay.

00:01:50.752 --> 00:01:51.585
- It's, like, two strings.

00:01:51.585 --> 00:01:52.431
- [LA] Yeah!

00:01:52.431 --> 00:01:55.885
Ding-a-dink-dink-dink-da-na-na-na-na

00:01:55.885 --> 00:01:56.718
♪ Controversy ♪

00:01:56.718 --> 00:01:58.290
"Controversy" and "Kiss" are the blueprint

00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.123
of that kinda stuff.

00:01:59.123 --> 00:02:00.750
And when you listened
to his rhythm playing.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:01.960
- But how did the sound evolve

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.670
in the relative isolation of Minneapolis?

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.890
To answer that, we have to
start with the music career

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.317
of another Prince, Prince Rogers.

00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:11.840
Prince Rogers was the stage name

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.034
of John Lewis Nelson, Prince's father.

00:02:14.034 --> 00:02:16.480
Nelson was one of the many black musicians

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:17.990
who migrated to the Twin Cities

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.301
in the 20th century from the South.

00:02:19.301 --> 00:02:22.220
Many of the them settled
here in my neighborhood,

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.960
North Minneapolis.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.538
There's my high school we're North High.

00:02:26.538 --> 00:02:29.220
The Polars, let's get it.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.420
They brought with them an
expertise in black genres

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.070
like blues, gospel, and jazz.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:35.840
Minneapolis had also long been home

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.410
to other musical traditions

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.152
from the polkas of European settlers

00:02:39.152 --> 00:02:41.400
to the Dinkytown Folk Music Circuit

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.250
where Bob Dillon would get his start.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:45.580
But segregation kept the
black and white music scenes

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.030
mostly separate during
the mid 20th century.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.600
In Minneapolis, if a
club became too black,

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.470
the police would find a
reason to shut it down.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.210
Well, that didn't stop local
black soul and funk bands

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.380
like Maurice McKinnies and The Champions,

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.400
Haze, Prophets of Peace,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.420
Jellybean's first band Flyte Tyme

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.080
and of course, Prince's
first band Grand Central

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.260
from creating a local black music scene.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.180
But because the black
population in Minneapolis

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.160
was still relatively small,
these musicians were absorbing

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.960
and playing other genres like
folk, rock and roll, and pop.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.680
- [Jellybean] My mom moved me here in 1986

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.220
to keep me out of the gangs in Chicago.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.410
- [LA] Hmm.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:22.350
- I came from Chicago

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.140
and I was around, you know, black radio.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:24.973
- Right.

00:03:24.973 --> 00:03:25.806
- All the time.

00:03:25.806 --> 00:03:27.590
And so when I got here, we
didn't have black radio,

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.986
black radio was only
on for like four hours.

00:03:29.986 --> 00:03:31.670
And so that meaned I had to listen to

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.590
the white rock stations and,
you know, the Three Dog Nights

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.100
and the Rare Earths, and Black Sabbath.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.483
You know, a young black
kid that, you know,

00:03:38.483 --> 00:03:39.510
that was kind of different.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.470
- Different, yeah.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.410
- I found out I started
to like it, you know.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:43.243
(laughing)

00:03:43.243 --> 00:03:45.630
And so it changed me musically, too.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.417
- These eclectic influences
merged on Prince's third album

00:03:49.417 --> 00:03:52.727
"Dirty Mind" which introduced
the Minneapolis sound.

00:03:52.727 --> 00:03:55.590
"Rolling Stone" called "Dirty
Mind" one of the most radical,

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:58.159
180 degree turns in pop history.

00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:01.063
The album is over the top
both in it's stylization

00:04:01.063 --> 00:04:02.987
and it's highly sexual lyrics.

00:04:02.987 --> 00:04:05.649
"Dirty Mind" found crossover
appeal on the national level

00:04:05.649 --> 00:04:08.540
by appealing not only
to the funk and R&amp;B fans

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:12.233
but also to punk rock and new wave crowds.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.301
In the early '80s, the Minneapolis
night club First Avenue

00:04:16.301 --> 00:04:18.870
was exploding with a mix of music, too.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:21.330
Punk bands like Husker
Du and The Replacements

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:23.270
were making a name for
themselves playing the club's

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.950
smaller, dirty stage at the 7th St. Entry

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.800
while Prince was packing the larger,

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.637
main room stage next door.

00:04:29.637 --> 00:04:32.760
Prince used this club to try out new music

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.180
by slipping unreleased
recordings to the DJ

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.000
and watching the crowd react.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.597
And when he was ready for
his feature film debut,

00:04:38.597 --> 00:04:41.670
he used First Avenue as
the band battle ground

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.208
in the film Purple Rain.

00:04:43.208 --> 00:04:44.160
(engine revving)

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.210
- He was the master of layering
them funky guitar parts,

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.300
you know, and he put them in there subtly.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.133
- Yeah.

00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:50.966
- You just hear them back in the back,

00:04:50.966 --> 00:04:52.583
they be brewing, you know,
got all this other stuff goin.

00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:53.665
- [LA] Plug it in.

00:04:53.665 --> 00:04:54.498
- He was a master of that.

00:04:54.498 --> 00:04:56.018
The guitar playing on "Lady
Cab Driver", the rhythm,

00:04:56.018 --> 00:04:58.686
I mean, go back, listen
to that, that's crazy.

00:04:58.686 --> 00:05:01.680
(Lady Cab Driver playing)

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.150
That's Minneapolis.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:04.290
- Smooth.
- That's crazy.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.179
- So in musical terms, what
is the Minneapolis sound?

00:05:07.179 --> 00:05:10.530
Some key ingredients include
highly processed instruments,

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:11.753
especially drums.

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:15.949
♪ Can I get some nasty bass. ♪

00:05:15.949 --> 00:05:18.616
(Human playing)

00:05:19.702 --> 00:05:23.150
(I Didn't Mean To Turn You On play)

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.210
And using synthesizers where prior bands

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:26.200
may have used horns.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.109
♪ Baby can you help me ♪

00:05:30.109 --> 00:05:32.100
- As kids, you know, I was lucky,

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.130
I was in Flyte Tyme and
we had like five horns.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.230
Well, Prince was in Grand
Central they didn't have horns.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.230
Then he heard our horns
and he's like, well,

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:39.292
we don't have a horn section.

00:05:39.292 --> 00:05:41.160
I know he thinks, man, he's cocky.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:41.993
Well, we don't have one

00:05:41.993 --> 00:05:43.517
but you know what I'm gonna fix that.

00:05:43.517 --> 00:05:44.560
(laughing)

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.210
- Was Flyte Tyme and Grand Central,

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:47.180
those were separate studios--

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.707
- We were, yeah, we
were like rival bands--

00:05:48.707 --> 00:05:49.540
- Separate brands, okay.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:50.560
- We were like rival bands at 15-year-old.

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:51.393
- Dope.

00:05:51.393 --> 00:05:52.226
- Dope, yeah.

00:05:52.226 --> 00:05:53.059
- Okay.

00:05:53.059 --> 00:05:55.230
Prince's "Dirty Mind"
wasn't a blow out success

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.730
in terms of record sales

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:58.320
but it paved the way for other bands

00:05:58.320 --> 00:05:59.540
to develop the sound.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.680
One of the first and most successful

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.868
was Jellybean's Band, The Time.

00:06:03.868 --> 00:06:06.701
♪ What time is it ♪

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:10.150
The Minneapolis band lead by Morris Day

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:11.806
was created by Prince in 1981.

00:06:11.806 --> 00:06:14.540
Like much of Prince's
work, The Time's music

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.798
was danceable, funky, highly processed,

00:06:16.798 --> 00:06:18.820
and used synthesizers.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:21.590
But unlike Prince who was known
for his androgynous style,

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:24.257
The Time's image was all
about dressing immaculately,

00:06:24.257 --> 00:06:27.913
with exotic suits, slick
haircuts, and shined shoes.

00:06:28.801 --> 00:06:29.760
(spitting)

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.230
- Hey, watch it now.

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:33.370
- In the mid '80s, two
members of The Time,

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.100
Jimmy Jam and Terry Lewis broke away

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.571
to create their own production company,

00:06:37.571 --> 00:06:39.458
Flyte Tyme Productions.

00:06:39.458 --> 00:06:41.150
Over the next decade,

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:43.310
they helped take the
Minneapolis sound platinum

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:46.570
with massive hits like Janet
Jackson's album "Control",

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:48.703
which they produced and helped to write.

00:06:48.703 --> 00:06:52.227
♪ What have you done for me lately ♪

00:06:52.227 --> 00:06:54.170
Jellybean Johnson also
worked with Jimmy and Terry.

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:55.506
- [Guys In The Back] Why Bean?

00:06:55.506 --> 00:06:57.498
- Did y'all see how Janet looked tonight?

00:06:57.498 --> 00:06:59.430
- Helping Janet rock even harder

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.711
with her 1989 hit "Black Cat."

00:07:01.711 --> 00:07:05.807
♪ All the lonely nights I stand alone ♪

00:07:05.807 --> 00:07:08.270
- "Black Cat," you know, it tripped out

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.930
all the heavy metal guys but
that's what I was listening to,

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.210
you know, and so I got some of my friends

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:16.280
and we rented a Marshall Amp,
and turned it up real loud,

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:17.532
kicked their butt out of Flyte Tyme.

00:07:17.532 --> 00:07:18.365
(laughing)

00:07:18.365 --> 00:07:19.198
- We rented the amp.

00:07:19.198 --> 00:07:20.031
(laughing)

00:07:20.031 --> 00:07:22.290
The Minneapolis sound of the '80s and '90s

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:24.599
helped to the Twin Cities
of Minneapolis and St. Paul

00:07:24.599 --> 00:07:26.803
into a great place to be a musician.

00:07:26.803 --> 00:07:29.060
The music didn't stop when Prince died,

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:31.730
there's a dynamic and
diverse local music scene

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:33.430
where an artist like Lizzo can perform

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:36.485
with a range of musicians all
while discovering her voice.

00:07:36.485 --> 00:07:38.096
♪ Can't ya feel that kick, kick ♪

00:07:38.096 --> 00:07:40.320
♪ Boom, boom, kick, kick, boom, boom ♪

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.116
All right, so for this
Minneapolis track that we created,

00:07:43.116 --> 00:07:46.530
I had Jellybean lay down three
separate layers of guitar.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:47.695
So here's the first joint.

00:07:47.695 --> 00:07:51.028
(funky electric guitar)

00:07:53.335 --> 00:07:54.950
That's the first layer,

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:57.961
now first and second layer put together.

00:07:57.961 --> 00:08:01.294
(funky electric guitar)

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:06.130
Third layer of the B
section, when we go the four,

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:07.160
let's play it by itself

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:08.970
'cause it's actually where the flavor is,

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:10.334
that's where the flavor

00:08:10.334 --> 00:08:12.751
♪ Mm, mm, mm ♪

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.470
It's so simple but it's so necessary.

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:28.920
The cool part about it is

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:31.810
it's tiny, little parts
all added together,

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:34.146
it's like a big puzzle
to make one soundscape.

00:08:34.146 --> 00:08:36.658
Down here we have some keyboard parts

00:08:36.658 --> 00:08:38.401
that have different roles
in the track as well.

00:08:38.401 --> 00:08:42.970
Some of these parts act like
horn parts or like right here,

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:46.160
brass sense solo, just so you can hear it.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:47.468
One, two.

00:08:47.468 --> 00:08:49.090
(synthesized horn sounds)

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:50.140
That's like a horn line.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.080
I was listening to a bunch
of Morris Day and The Time,

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:53.790
a bunch of Prince, I realized that

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:56.270
they weren't playing
these full bodied chords

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:58.471
but it was more so about the rhythm in it.

00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:00.280
♪ Blee, blip, blu, blip, blip ♪

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.588
Simple, step away from it, come back.

00:09:02.588 --> 00:09:04.010
♪ Buhm, da, da ♪

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:05.080
Simple.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:08.150
(synthesized horn sounds)

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:11.262
That's kinda how I use that
synth right there for the horns.

00:09:11.262 --> 00:09:14.480
I'm not a pianist, I'm
not a keys player at all

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:18.210
but I feel like, you
know, the soul is there,

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:19.630
the groove is there, the vibe is there.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:21.510
That's really what the music was about,

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:22.410
it was about the feel.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:25.383
All together, this is what it sounds like.

00:09:26.255 --> 00:09:29.338
(funky upbeat music)

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.560
The Minneapolis sound
did more for the culture

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.640
that just leave an
imprint on Top 40 music.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.620
It was a unique fusion of genres

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:40.840
that helped break down barriers,

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:42.750
not only between musical traditions

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:45.050
but also between different
racial communities.

00:09:46.897 --> 00:09:50.070
All though Minneapolis is
still no racial utopia,

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:52.380
the Minneapolis sound has
brought different people together

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:54.290
on the dance floor for decades.

00:09:58.452 --> 00:10:00.535
I forgot about this part.

00:10:52.377 --> 00:10:54.395
Hey, love watching PBS shows?

00:10:54.395 --> 00:10:57.040
Well, the PBS Video App is your home

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:58.850
for amazing educational shows

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.653
ranging from science films on Nova

00:11:00.653 --> 00:11:02.621
to music on Austin City Limits,

00:11:02.621 --> 00:11:05.440
to documentaries from
Ken Burns and Frontline.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:07.232
All with no ads or algorithms.

00:11:07.232 --> 00:11:09.990
Oh, and Sound Field, we're there, too.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:12.640
If you live in the US, check
out the link in our description

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:16.063
